CAT = "ukfawResus"; SEG = "RES"
Q = [
("Under the 2025 UK guidelines, when should you call 999 for a collapsed adult?",
 "Once they are unresponsive", ["After one full minute of CPR", "Once you have found no pulse", "After checking for injuries"],
 "Resuscitation Council UK's 2025 guidelines say to call 999 for any unresponsive person, then assess breathing while the call connects. You no longer need to confirm abnormal breathing before calling."),
("For how long should you check an unresponsive adult for normal breathing?",
 "Up to 10 seconds", ["About 30 seconds", "At least one minute", "Up to 20 seconds"],
 "Look, listen and feel for no more than 10 seconds. A longer check delays CPR, and if you are in any doubt you should treat the breathing as abnormal."),
("How do you normally open the airway of an unresponsive adult with no suspected neck injury?",
 "Head tilt, chin lift", ["Jaw thrust only", "Lift the neck up", "Pull the tongue forward"],
 "Tilting the head back and lifting the chin lifts the tongue off the back of the throat. Jaw thrust is kept for a suspected spinal injury."),
("An unresponsive adult is making slow, occasional gasps. How should you treat this?",
 "As not breathing normally", ["As breathing normally", "As a sign of choking", "As a faint that will pass"],
 "Occasional gasps (agonal breathing) are common in the first minutes of cardiac arrest. Treat them as abnormal breathing and start CPR."),
("How deep should chest compressions be on an adult?",
 "5 to 6 cm", ["2 to 3 cm", "3 to 4 cm", "7 to 8 cm"],
 "The UK guidelines say to compress at least 5 cm but not more than 6 cm. Shallower compressions move too little blood; deeper ones risk injury."),
("At what rate should you give adult chest compressions?",
 "100 to 120 a minute", ["60 to 80 a minute", "80 to 100 a minute", "120 to 140 a minute"],
 "Compress at 100 to 120 a minute with as few interruptions as possible. Going faster than 120 tends to make compressions shallower."),
("What ratio of compressions to rescue breaths do you use for an adult?",
 "30:2", ["15:2", "30:5", "5:1"],
 "Adult CPR is 30 compressions followed by 2 rescue breaths. 15:2 is the ratio used in paediatric CPR."),
("Where do you place your hands for adult chest compressions?",
 "Lower half of the breastbone", ["Upper half of the breastbone", "Left side over the heart", "Just below the ribcage"],
 "Place the heel of one hand on the lower half of the breastbone in the centre of the chest, with the other hand on top. Pressing below the ribcage risks injuring the organs underneath."),
("You are unable or unwilling to give rescue breaths. What should you do?",
 "Give continuous compressions", ["Give rescue breaths only", "Wait for the ambulance", "Put them in recovery position"],
 "Compression-only CPR is far better than no CPR. Keep compressing at 100 to 120 a minute without stopping until help or an AED arrives."),
("How much air should each rescue breath give an adult?",
 "Enough to make the chest rise", ["As much as you can force in", "One quick, short, sharp puff", "Enough to puff out the cheeks"],
 "Blow steadily for about a second, just enough to make the chest start to rise. Forceful breaths push air into the stomach and make vomiting more likely."),
("About how long should each rescue breath take?",
 "About 1 second", ["About 3 seconds", "About 5 seconds", "Half a second"],
 "Each breath should take about one second. Longer breaths waste time between compressions and over-inflate the casualty."),
("When should you stop CPR on an adult?",
 "When they breathe normally", ["After 10 minutes of CPR", "After five cycles of 30:2", "When the AED says no shock"],
 "Keep going until the casualty shows signs of life and breathes normally, a professional takes over or you are exhausted. A no-shock message does not mean the heart has restarted."),
("The AED says no shock is advised. What should you do next?",
 "Restart CPR at once", ["Stop CPR and wait", "Remove the AED pads", "Switch the AED off"],
 "Restart compressions immediately and leave the pads on. The AED will reanalyse the rhythm about every two minutes and prompt you."),
("The AED is analysing the heart rhythm. What must you do?",
 "Make sure no one touches them", ["Carry on with chest compressions", "Give two rescue breaths", "Feel for a carotid pulse"],
 "Movement during analysis can confuse the AED, and touching the casualty during a shock is dangerous. Stand clear, then restart CPR as soon as it tells you."),
("Where do AED pads go?",
 "On the bare chest", ["Over thin clothing", "Both on the back", "On the upper arms"],
 "Pads must stick to bare skin in the positions shown on the pads or AED. Clothing stops the shock reaching the heart properly."),
("The casualty's chest is wet with rain. What should you do before applying the AED pads?",
 "Dry the chest first", ["Put pads over the wet", "Wait until it dries", "Use only one pad"],
 "Wipe the chest dry quickly so the pads stick and the shock passes through the heart rather than across wet skin. Do not delay waiting for it to dry on its own."),
("A medication patch is where an AED pad needs to go. What should you do?",
 "Remove it, wipe the skin", ["Place the pad on top of it", "Do not use the AED at all", "Move the pad to the neck"],
 "Peel off the patch and wipe the area so the pad sits on clean skin. A pad over a patch can reduce the shock and cause burns."),
("You see a lump under the skin from an implanted pacemaker. Where do you put the AED pad?",
 "Away from the lump", ["Directly over the lump", "On the left upper arm", "Nowhere, skip the AED"],
 "Place the pad clear of the implanted device and use the AED as normal. A pacemaker is no reason to withhold defibrillation."),
("Who can use a public access AED?",
 "Anyone", ["Only first-aiders", "Only paramedics", "Only adults over 21"],
 "Public access AEDs talk the user through each step, so anyone can use one. Resuscitation Council UK stresses that you need no training to try."),
("Two rescuers are giving CPR. How often should they swap who does compressions?",
 "About every 2 minutes", ["Every 30 seconds", "Every 10 minutes", "Only if one is injured"],
 "Swap about every two minutes, ideally while the AED is analysing, because compression quality drops as rescuers tire."),
("Which casualty should be placed in the recovery position?",
 "Unresponsive, breathing normally", ["Unresponsive, not breathing normally", "Alert with a cut to the hand", "Choking but still coughing well"],
 "The recovery position keeps the airway open and lets fluid drain from the mouth. A casualty who is not breathing normally needs CPR instead."),
("In the recovery position, what supports the casualty's cheek?",
 "Back of their upper hand", ["Palm of their lower hand", "A folded coat or a bag", "Their own bent elbow"],
 "The back of the upper hand goes against the cheek to support the head and keep the airway open. The lower arm lies out at a right angle."),
("Where should a heavily pregnant woman be placed in the recovery position?",
 "On her left side", ["On her right side", "Flat on her back", "Face down"],
 "The left side keeps the weight of the womb off the large vein that returns blood to the heart. Lying flat on her back can cut her blood pressure."),
("A casualty is in the recovery position awaiting the ambulance. What should you do?",
 "Keep checking their breathing", ["Give them small sips of water", "Leave them to rest alone", "Sit them up every minute"],
 "Monitor breathing continuously and be ready to start CPR if it stops being normal. Nothing should be given by mouth to someone with reduced responsiveness."),
("An unresponsive adult was pulled from water and is not breathing. How should CPR begin?",
 "With 5 rescue breaths", ["With 30 compressions", "With 2 rescue breaths", "With 5 back blows"],
 "Drowning is a breathing problem, so the UK guidance starts with five rescue breaths before moving on to 30:2. Back blows do not clear water from the lungs."),
("Why should you let the chest come fully back up after each compression?",
 "To let the heart refill", ["To rest your arms a little", "To check for a pulse", "To open the airway"],
 "Full recoil lets blood flow back into the heart so the next compression has blood to push. Leaning on the chest reduces that refill."),
("What is the first link in the chain of survival?",
 "Early recognition, call for help", ["Early CPR to buy some time", "Early defibrillation with an AED", "Good care after resuscitation"],
 "The chain starts with recognising cardiac arrest and calling 999, then early CPR, early defibrillation and post-resuscitation care. Every later link depends on the first."),
("The casualty vomits during CPR. What should you do?",
 "Turn them, clear mouth, restart", ["Carry on without pausing at all", "Stop CPR and wait for help", "Give five abdominal thrusts"],
 "Turn the casualty towards you, let the vomit drain and clear the mouth, then roll them back and restart CPR quickly. Breaths into a blocked mouth will not work."),
("What is the name for infrequent, noisy gasps in an unresponsive person?",
 "Agonal breathing", ["Hyperventilation", "Wheezing", "Stridor"],
 "Agonal breathing is a sign of cardiac arrest, not of normal breathing. Wheezing and stridor are noises from a narrowed airway in a breathing person."),
("Besides 999, which number reaches the emergency services in the UK?",
 "112", ["111", "101", "118"],
 "112 works the same as 999 across the UK and Europe. 111 is the NHS non-emergency line and 101 is the police non-emergency line."),
("Why put your phone on speaker when you call 999 for a collapsed casualty?",
 "To hear the call handler", ["To save the phone's battery", "To record the whole call", "To let bystanders hear"],
 "On speaker you can keep your hands free for CPR while the call handler counts and coaches you. The 2025 UK guidelines recommend it."),
("Why use a face shield when giving rescue breaths?",
 "To reduce infection risk", ["To make breaths go deeper", "To keep the head tilted", "To stop the casualty vomiting"],
 "A face shield or pocket mask puts a barrier between you and the casualty. It does not improve the breath or prevent vomiting."),
]
